anyway, so I'll go with the straightforward approach and match GNU cat's format as closely as possible.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the overall structure of the implementation and what imports and packages I'll need.\n\nI'm defining the options struct to hold all the command-line flags, and a catState struct to track line numbering and blank line state for the squeeze feature. Then in main, I'm setting up signal handling for SIGPIPE to exit cleanly, and starting to parse the command-line flags for all the different options like number lines, number non-blank lines, squeeze blank lines, and show non-printing characters.\n\nParsing command-line flags...\n\nNow I'm handling the combined flag options like `-A` for showing all non-printing characters, then setting up the file list to read from stdin if no files are provided, and initializing the output buffer and state tracker before processing each file.\n\nContinuing with the file processing loop... I need to be careful with how `ReadBytes` works—it includes the delimiter, and if there's no trailing newline, it returns the data with `io.EOF`. Now I'm implementing the `writeLine` function to handle the actual line processing, checking whether each line ends with a newline and detecting blank lines so I can apply the squeeze option correctly.\n\nWriting line processing logic...\n\nActually, I'm noticing a potential issue with how blank lines interact with the `-b` and `-E` flags. Let me trace through what GNU cat does when both are active—specifically whether it adds the end-of-line marker to blank lines or just outputs the newline.\n\nLooking at the transformation order, `-E` applies before `-b`, so the `$` gets added to all lines that have newlines, but the line number from `-b` only appears on non-blank lines. The key insight is that adding `$` doesn't change whether a line counts as blank for numbering purposes.\n\nNow I'm implementing the `displayByte` function... I'm realizing there's a nuance with how the M- prefi